<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diyar Bakr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Diyar Bakr province</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diyar Bakr was the medieval Arabic name for the northernmost province of the Jazira, the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> basin around Amid (modern Diyarbakır) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>. Named after the Arab tribe of Bakr ibn Wa'il, it was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> before the Kurdish Marwanid dynasty ruled it from the 980s to the 1080s, when it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Diyar Bakr, meaning abode of Bakr, was the northernmost of the three provinces of the Jazira, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>, the others being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar">Diyar Mudar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-rabia">Diyar Rabi'a</a>. The medieval geographer al-Baladhuri held that all three were named after the Arab tribes settled there by Mu'awiya during the Muslim conquests of the 7th century. Diyar Bakr was settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a</a> subgroup of the Banu Bakr, so the two provinces are sometimes called Diyar Rabi'a together.</p><p>The province covered both banks of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, from its sources to about where the river turns from an eastward to a south-eastward course. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>, its main city, it included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>, Hisn Kaifa and Arzan. In early Islamic times it was usually part of the Jazira, but it was sometimes joined to the Armenian province to the north. In later Turkish usage the name came to mean only the western part around Amid, which became known as Diyarbakır.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late 9th century the region was held by an autonomous dynasty founded by Isa ibn al-Shaykh al-Shaybani, and in the mid-10th century it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a>. After the Marwanid emirate, it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> in 1084–1085. When the Seljuk realm broke up after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah I</a> in 1092, small emirates took over the region, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a> the most important of them.</p><p>In the late 12th century the province came under Ayyubid control, and by the mid-13th century it was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> in the east and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a> in the west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered it around 1260 and let local rulers govern as their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. In the 14th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> took the region, disputing it with the Qara Qoyunlu and the last Ayyubid princes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> occupied it for a time in the early 16th century before it came under Ottoman control in 1516.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diyar Bakr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 980s the Kurdish chief Badh took advantage of Buyid weakness to seize Mayyafariqin, Amid and much of <b>Diyar Bakr</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/marwanids">Marwanids</a></li></ul></section>
